<article class="env-exercise" id="02B0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02B0">Exercise <span data-tag="02B0">111.52.5</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a projective, reduced scheme over $k$. Let $f : X \to \mathbf{P}^1_ k$ be a morphism of schemes over $k$. Assume there exists an integer $d \geq 0$ such that for every point $t \in \mathbf{P}^1_ k$ the fibre $X_ t = f^{-1}(t)$ is irreducible of dimension $d$. (Recall that an irreducible space is not empty.) </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Show that $\dim (X) = d + 1$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Let $X_0 \subset X$ be an irreducible component of $X$ of dimension $d + 1$. Prove that for every $t \in \mathbf{P}^1_ k$ the fibre $X_{0, t}$ has dimension $d$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
What can you conclude about $X_ t$ and $X_{0, t}$ from the above? 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that $X$ is irreducible. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>